diagnosis_text:
Comprehensive end-to-end testing of the PC0-to-PC12 path reveals no fault. PC0 (20.1.1.10/8) reaches PC12 (120.1.1.10/8) bidirectionally with zero packet loss, and the four-hop traceroute resolves all intermediate routers cleanly: PC0 → r1 (20.1.1.1) → r11 (111.1.1.2) → r12 (112.1.1.2) → PC12.

The routing plane is fully consistent. r1 holds a valid OSPF external route to 120.0.0.0/8 via r11 (distance 110, metric 20, installed in FIB). r11 is the EIGRP-to-OSPF redistribution boundary: its FRR config carries `redistribute eigrp` under `router ospf`, and the OSPF external LSA for 120.0.0.0/8 (mask /8, Type-2, metric 20, advertising router 112.1.1.1) is present in the OSPF database. r12's EIGRP config explicitly includes `network 120.0.0.0/8`, so the prefix enters EIGRP, is learned by r11, and redistributed into OSPF Area 3 toward r1. The return path is symmetric: r12 has a default route to r11, r11 has OSPF route 20.0.0.0/8 via r1, and r1 directly connects PC0 on eth4. No iptables filter rules exist on r1 beyond Docker-internal DNS NAT, which does not affect transit traffic. All OSPF adjacencies (r1–r11, r11–r1) are in Full state.

The user's reported symptom — complete unreachability between PC0 and PC12 — is not reproducible on the live lab. The network is operating correctly. No corrective action is needed.
